 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery.** Committed edits are pushed to browsers over server-managed WebSocket sessions. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected because per-request overhead and polling lag become disruptive at opening-time burst rates (20 edits per second reaching 800 browsers). A hosted realtime service was rejected to avoid recurring vendor fees and provider coupling. Self-hosted WebSockets deliver sub-second push without a realtime vendor, at the cost of owning connection capacity, session state, and reconnect logic. See `docs/decisions/delivery-mechanism.md`.
